$DIR/main.p:17:9: cannot return &n.val, which points into the object n refers to, which n keeps alive in field
    &n.val points into the object n refers to, which n keeps alive in field. n
    (declared at main.p:16) is an owned[Node]: the object it refers to lives
    only while n holds it, and is dropped when n is dropped at the end of its
    scope in field, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into
    the object, or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive n.
    When field returns, n is dropped and the object freed, so &n.val would point
    at freed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return n itself: an owned box moves to the caller, and a shared or synced
    one gives the caller its own reference:
        return n
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:23:2: cannot store (*Node)(n) in package variable kept, which outlives n
    (*Node)(n) points into the object n refers to, which n keeps alive in keep.
    n (declared at main.p:22) is an owned[Node]: the object it refers to lives
    only while n holds it, and is dropped when n is dropped at the end of its
    scope in keep, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the
    object, or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive n.
    When keep returns, n is dropped and the object freed, but package variables
    live for the whole program, so package variable kept would be left pointing
    at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store n itself, changing the type of kept to match: an owned box moves
    there, and a shared or synced one is counted:
        kept = n
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:29:9: cannot return n as an interface: an ordinary interface only borrows the box, which is dropped when lend returns
    n points into the object n refers to, which n keeps alive in lend. n
    (declared at main.p:28) is an owned[Node]: the object it refers to lives
    only while n holds it, and is dropped when n is dropped at the end of its
    scope in lend, or released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the
    object, or an interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive n.
    Converting n to an ordinary interface lends it: the interface points at the
    object, and n still owns it.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the box itself, changing the result type to owned[Node]:
        return n
    Or declare the interface as an owning interface, which takes ownership of
    what is converted into it (language spec section 18.5):
        type I owner interface {
            ...
        }
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:34:9: cannot return (*Node)(new(Node)), which points into the object of a temporary owned[Node] in temp's frame
    (*Node)(new(Node)) points into the object of a temporary owned[Node] in
    temp's frame. The owned[Node] made at main.p:34 is a temporary: nothing
    keeps it, so its object is dropped at the end of the statement, and a view
    of the object may not outlive that statement. At the end of the statement,
    the temporary is dropped and its object freed, so (*Node)(new(Node)) would
    point at freed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the box itself, changing the result type to owned[Node]:
        return new(Node)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
